@page "/dialog-submit"

<PageTitle>Dialog-first submit</PageTitle>
<h1>Dialog-first submit</h1>
<p>This page announces a blocked submit with a dialog: everything that needs attention, in one place, in front of the form. Click a name in the dialog's list and the dialog closes before focus lands on that field.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "InvoiceRequest.cs", "DialogSubmit.razor", "DialogSubmit.razor.cs", "AnnouncementDialog.razor", "AnnouncementDialog.razor.cs" })">
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong> with every box empty.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> a dialog over the form, headed "This form is not ready to send", with focus on the dialog itself.</div></li>
            <li>Read the list in the dialog.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> "6 fields need attention", four field names and "2 more to fix" under them, with <strong>Invoice reference</strong> named once.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> The dialog's summary sets <code>GroupByField</code>: one entry per field, however many rules it breaks.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Invoice reference</strong> in the list.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the dialog fades out, then focus lands in <strong>Invoice reference</strong>, ready to type, with both its messages under the box.</div></li>
            <li>Untick <strong>Wait for the dialog to finish closing before moving focus</strong>, click <strong>Submit</strong>, then click any name.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the box takes focus, then loses it to <strong>Submit</strong>.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> Unticked, focus moves before the dialog closes, and closing hands it back to <strong>Submit</strong>.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Wait for the dialog to finish closing before moving focus</strong> again, click <strong>Submit</strong>, then press Escape.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the dialog closes, and focus lands in <strong>Invoice reference</strong>.</div><div class="teaching__why"><span class="teaching__label">Why:</span> Escape names no field, so the page asks the form to focus the first error.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Submit</strong>, then click <strong>Close</strong> in the dialog.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> focus lands in <strong>Invoice reference</strong> again.</div></li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Focus the first error automatically on a blocked submit</strong> and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> the dialog opens, and focus ends in <strong>Invoice reference</strong> behind it, with the blue focus border.</div></li>
            <li>Click <strong>Close</strong>, untick that toggle, fill each box (<code>INV-2031</code>, a valid email, an amount above zero, anything elsewhere) and click <strong>Submit</strong>.<div class="teaching__result"><span class="teaching__label">You see:</span> no dialog; "Submitted" in the status line.</div></li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Invoice reference</strong>: required; shaped like <code>INV-0000</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Supplier</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Supplier email</strong>: required; a valid email address.</li>
            <li><strong>Cost centre</strong>: required.</li>
            <li><strong>Amount</strong>: above zero.</li>
            <li><strong>Description</strong>: required.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <HowItWorks>
        <p>The dialog is the page's own: the library ships no styling and no dialog. What Formidable brings is the list inside it, and the ride from a name in that list to the field it belongs to.</p>
        <p>The two toggles above the form start in the setting this page wants. Flipping either one gives it a setting that does not work, and that is what they are for: you watch the failure instead of taking the page's word for it. Flip one, walk the step that names it, watch focus end up somewhere nobody chose, and flip it back.</p>
        <p>The <a href="summary-shape">Shaping the summary</a> page has the other kind of toggle. Each sets a parameter a real form could ship either way, so you choose what the list says, not whether it works. The summary in this dialog sets four of those parameters at once; that page shows each on its own.</p>
        <p>Opening the dialog is the job of the page's own <code>OnInvalidSubmit</code> handler. The line under the dialog's heading counts fields, not complaints. It reads <code>context.Outcome.VisibleErrorSummary</code>, the submit's list of the distinct names of the errors showing, so the count is one per field.</p>
        <p>A blocked submit moves focus to the first error on the page, straight after <code>OnInvalidSubmit</code> returns and inside the same submit. With the dialog over the form, that would put the caret in a box the overlay covers, where a keystroke lands unseen. So the handler calls <code>context.SuppressFirstErrorFocus()</code> as it opens the dialog, the one place that knows a dialog is coming. A form whose handler only logs something wants the move to happen.</p>
        <p>Ticking <strong>Focus the first error automatically on a blocked submit</strong> skips that call, so the failure is something to watch rather than something to take on trust. Ticked, the dialog takes focus as it finishes opening, and the form's move then takes it to <strong>Invoice reference</strong> behind the overlay.</p>
        <p><code>PrepareFocus</code> is a callback the summary awaits before it moves focus, so the page can clear the way first. Here the callback closes the dialog and waits for the close to finish. On its way out, the dialog hands focus back to the button that opened it, which would take back a move made any earlier. Unticking <strong>Wait for the dialog to finish closing before moving focus</strong> makes the callback report ready at once instead.</p>
        <p><code>FocusFirstErrorAsync()</code> asks the form for the move a blocked submit makes for itself: focus to the first error. The page calls it as the dialog goes. <strong>Close</strong> and Escape name no field, so no entry is clicked and the summary sends nobody anywhere. Without this call, the dialog's hand-back would leave you on the button you pressed, looking for the first problem by eye.</p>
        <p>So closing the dialog without choosing a name lands you where the form would have put you had the dialog never opened. <strong>Close</strong> and Escape wait for the dialog to finish closing, its hand-back to <strong>Submit</strong> included, and only then does the page call <code>FocusFirstErrorAsync()</code>. The page's move comes last, so it wins whichever way the wait toggle is set.</p>
        <p>The list's own settings: <code>ItemTemplate</code> makes each entry a field name instead of a message, <code>GroupByField</code> gives one entry per field, and <code>MaxItems</code> with <code>OverflowTemplate</code> caps the list at four and says how many are left. A dialog is a small space, and a form with thirty problems still has to fit inside one.</p>
        <p><strong>Invoice reference</strong> has two rules, and an empty box breaks both. A list of names has to collapse that pair into one entry, and a list of messages has to keep both. The field itself keeps both messages; only the list names it once.</p>
    </HowItWorks>
    <Demo>
        <div class="toggles">
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_waitForClose" /> Wait for the dialog to finish closing before moving focus</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_autoFocusFirstError" /> Focus the first error automatically on a blocked submit</label>
        </div>

        <FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_request" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid" OnInvalidSubmit="AnnounceAsync">
            <div class="field"><label>Invoice reference <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.Reference" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.Reference" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.Reference" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Supplier <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.SupplierName" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.SupplierName" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.SupplierName" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Supplier email <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.SupplierEmail" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.SupplierEmail" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.SupplierEmail" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Cost centre <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.CostCentre" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.CostCentre" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.CostCentre" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Amount <FormidableInputNumber @bind-Value="_request.Amount" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.Amount" /></div>
            <div class="field"><label>Description <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.Description" /> <FormidableInputTextArea @bind-Value="_request.Description" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.Description" /></div>

            <div class="actions">
                <button type="submit" @ref="_submitButton">Submit</button>
            </div>

            @* Inside the form on purpose: FormidableSummary reads the cascaded form context and throws
               when it is rendered outside a FormidableForm or FormidableValidator ancestor. The summary
               is inside the dialog for the other half of the reason — the entries a visitor can click
               have to be the ones in front of the overlay. *@
            <AnnouncementDialog @ref="_announcement" Heading="This form is not ready to send"
                                ReturnFocusTo="_submitButton" OnDismissed="ReturnToFirstErrorAsync">
                <p>@_blockedFields @(_blockedFields == 1 ? "field needs" : "fields need") attention. Pick one to go straight to it.</p>

                @* The entries are field names here rather than messages. DisplayName is the name
                   SubmitOutcome.VisibleErrorSummary lists the entry under, the one the line above
                   counts: the rule's own name (its WithName(...), or FluentValidation's name for the
                   property), else the property path, else ModelLevelDisplayName for an issue about the
                   whole form. *@
                <FormidableSummary Show="SummaryFilter.Errors" GroupByField="true" MaxItems="4"
                                   PrepareFocus="DismissAnnouncementAsync">
                    <ItemTemplate Context="entry">@entry.DisplayName</ItemTemplate>
                    <OverflowTemplate Context="held">@held.Count more to fix</OverflowTemplate>
                </FormidableSummary>
            </AnnouncementDialog>
        </FormidableForm>

        <p role="status">@_status</p>
    </Demo>
</TeachingPanel>
